 picture and that by  itself drew me in and and made this into  a mode of thought and a body of work  that I wanted to keep working on I'm  working out of working out because it  opened up more and more interesting  topics and questions more than I can  probably explore in my entire lifetime  so that's how I got into Aristotle and  then I started writing papers on  Aristotle and some of them were  presented at conferences a few of them  have been published here and there I  think it published about four or five  things on Aristotle so far and  eventually I decided I wanted to start  writing a book on Aristotle in his  discussions of anger bringing all these  these threads that I see in about well  nine or ten different texts all of them  together and and talking about it as a  systematic theory as best as one can and  then comparing it to other people's  discussions and theories and treatments  of  I'm also very interested in Aristotle's  conception of shared common goods and  political life and culture and moral  perception and so I do some writing on  those sorts of things I'm very  interested in Aristotle's metaphysical  scheme as well although I don't  necessarily buy into to all of it the  way that you know somebody who calls  himself Sania Aristotelian might do but  I think that he came up with some some  really useful ways to understand a lot  of key things and I think that if he had  a few more tools in his belt if he'd  come on the scene a bit later  his works might have been yet even more  powerful than incorporating a lot of  things so Aristotle is is an important  thinker and there's there's something  you know use a respectable thinker to  focus on anyway if you're in philosophy  and somebody says well what kind of  stuff do you do you it's always a safe  bet to say I'm an Aristotle scholar  because everybody knows who he is and  even if they don't like Aristotle at  least he knows part of that part of the  Canon and you're doing some sort of  reputable business that's different than  saying something like well I'm a Lacan  scholar which may not go over too well  in many circles or I'm interested in  Maurice blown down on that everyone says  Maurice who or you know Saint Anselm  scholar everyone thinks that you work on  the ontological argument all the time  why is Aristotle such a such an  important figure well you know we have  this kind of conception now of the  history of philosophy as all these these  periods and all these thinkers and and  there's sort of like canonical figures  like you know Socrates Plato Aristotle  moving forward you know Epictetus seneca  at the Kuras of course a little bit  earlier  sexist empirica cicero you know move  forward all the way to the to the  present and then it gets a little bit  you know more confused what who counts  as a canonical figure you know dick and  stein definitely but you know what about  Carnap you know or who counts and  continental philosophy definitely  Heidegger but what about Baudrillard you  know those are those are interesting  questions but let's put that up so  Aristotle is is now you know sort of an  established figure but there were a lot  of times where he was not studied and  I'm not just talking about you know  during the early Middle Ages when he'd  been lost he wasn't completely lost by  the way to the medievals did know about  him in part through glosses that were  available they lost a lot of his tax and  and later on recovered them through  through the Islamic world that through  these translations that are made  available but it's not as if he was  completely off the scene but he has been  in and out of vogue this is one of the  really interesting things about  Aristotle and Aristotelian philosophy in  some centuries Aristotelian ism was a  dirty word and it might actually be a  good word and a dirty word at the same  time like you know during the 13th  century reception of Aristotle in the in  the universities there are some people  who are